<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="robots" content="noindex,nofollow"><title>Paper C · Learner paper</title><base href="/api/v1/ap-chinese/2027/c/file/"><link rel="stylesheet" href="paper.css"><body><main class="page teacher"><section class="hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">HSKKit · AP Chinese 2027 · Original practice</p><h1>Paper C · Learner paper</h1><p class="lead">55 MCQs + four free-response tasks. Speaking uses an independent music-research scenario; the official 2027 project packet is not yet released.</p><div class="links"><a href="index.html">Practice workspace</a><a href="student.pdf">Learner PDF · 简体</a><a href="student-traditional.pdf">Learner PDF · 繁體</a><a href="teacher.pdf">Answer PDF</a><a href="listening.html">Audio review</a></div></div></section><div class="box"><h2>Before speaking: prepare your own research</h2><p>Before speaking, investigate how a musical tradition in a Chinese-speaking cultural context relates to a place or social occasion. Consult at least two identifiable sources. Explain a practice/perspective relationship, compare contexts, and distinguish documented information from interpretation. These source leads support independent research, not the unreleased official 2027 project packet.</p><p><a href="https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/hua-er-00211">UNESCO: Hua’er</a></p><p class="cn">UNESCO 的花儿条目介绍了甘肃、青海等地多个民族群体共享的歌唱传统。歌词可按约定方式即兴发挥，表达日常经历、个人情感等；歌唱既可能发生在劳作和路途中，也有花儿节等正式场合。可以研究：同一传统进入不同场合时，参与者、听众和表达机会怎样变化？这些是进一步研究的问题，不是条目已经证明的统一答案。</p><p><a href="https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/urtiin-duu-traditional-folk-long-song-00115">UNESCO: Urtiin Duu, traditional folk long song</a></p><p class="cn">UNESCO 的长调条目介绍了中国与蒙古国共同列入名录的音乐传统，提到婚礼等重要庆祝场合、与牧业生活的联系，以及不同地区的风格。可以继续研究：在一种具体场合里，演唱怎样参与人与人的关系？资料涉及的区域和场合有范围，不能据此认为所有演出都相同，也不能把一次舞台录像当成全部生活实践。</p></div><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 1</p><h2>Project presentation</h2><p>Present your research on a musical tradition and its relationship to a place or social occasion in a Chinese-speaking cultural context. Explain a specific practice and a cultural perspective using identifiable sources. Compare a familiar setting or reflect on what could change in another setting, and acknowledge a limit of your evidence. Speak in Chinese.</p><p class="cn">请介绍你研究的一种音乐传统，以及它与华语文化情境中某个地点或社会场合的关系。用可辨认来源说明具体实践与文化观念的联系，比较一种熟悉的情境，或反思换一个场合可能发生的变化，并指出证据的局限。请用中文作答。</p><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 11 points maximum: 任务完成 2, 文化理解 2, 组织 2, 语言控制 3, 表达呈现 2. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 2</p><h2>Project Q&A</h2><p>Answer four questions in Chinese about your research on music, place, and occasion. Each question is heard twice, followed by forty seconds to respond. All four responses receive one analytic review together.</p><p class="muted">Four recorded questions. Each prompt plays twice, then allows 40 seconds for a response. Open the practice workspace or audio review to hear them.</p><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 9 points maximum: 任务完成 2, 文化理解 2, 语言控制 3, 流利度 2. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 3</p><h2>Story narration</h2><p>Write a complete story in Chinese using all four pictures in order. Explain the loss, the actions that follow, and the resolution. You may choose names and add plausible details consistent with the pictures.</p><p class="cn">请按顺序使用全部四幅图，用中文写一个完整故事，交代物品遗失、之后的行动与解决结果。可以自定人物姓名，并补充符合画面顺序的合理细节。</p><div class="story-grid"><figure><img src="assets/images/story-1.svg" alt="A person in a purple coat with a gold bag leaves a bus-stop bench; a red scarf remains on the bench."><figcaption>Picture 1</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="assets/images/story-2.svg" alt="A student in a teal coat discovers the red scarf on the bench after its owner has left."><figcaption>Picture 2</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="assets/images/story-3.svg" alt="The student gives the red scarf to a blue-uniformed staff member at a lost-property desk."><figcaption>Picture 3</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="assets/images/story-4.svg" alt="The owner in the purple coat and gold bag reclaims the red scarf at the desk; the student and staff member are present."><figcaption>Picture 4</figcaption></figure></div><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 8 points maximum: 叙事展开 3, 组织 2, 语言控制 3. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box task"><p class="eyebrow">Free response 4</p><h2>Email response</h2><p>Reply in Chinese to the film-club coordinator. Address the requests, explain your choices, and ask a relevant preparation question. Use an appropriate email format.</p><p class="cn">校园电影社｜放映后的交流
同学你好！我们下月准备放映几部学生拍摄的生活短片，希望观众看完后能提出有帮助的意见，而不只是给作品排个名次。你建议用什么方式收集和交流反馈？请说明理由，以及你愿意负责哪项工作。
有些观众不习惯当众发言，中文表达能力也不同，你会怎样让他们参与？如果两位观众对同一段画面的理解相反，你建议怎样处理这些意见？最后，请提出一个开始准备前需要向我们确认的问题。
期待你的回复！
协调员 苏老师</p><p class="muted">Manual analytic review · 8 points maximum: 信息展开 3, 组织 2, 语言控制 3. No automatic grade.</p></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L1 · Questions 1–5</p><h2>Choosing a recording room</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l1.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>校园媒体中心录音室预约表</caption><thead><tr><th>房间</th><th>人数上限</th><th>单次使用上限</th><th>每次费用</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>青禾室</td><td>2人</td><td>30分钟</td><td>免费</td></tr><tr><td>竹影室</td><td>3人</td><td>60分钟</td><td>20元</td></tr><tr><td>松风室</td><td>5人</td><td>60分钟</td><td>20元</td></tr><tr><td>榆林室</td><td>6人</td><td>90分钟</td><td>35元</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">本表及收费规则均为原创模拟情境。</p><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l1.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>校園媒體中心錄音室預約表</caption><thead><tr><th>房間</th><th>人數上限</th><th>單次使用上限</th><th>每次費用</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>青禾室</td><td>2人</td><td>30分鐘</td><td>免費</td></tr><tr><td>竹影室</td><td>3人</td><td>60分鐘</td><td>20元</td></tr><tr><td>松風室</td><td>5人</td><td>60分鐘</td><td>20元</td></tr><tr><td>榆林室</td><td>6人</td><td>90分鐘</td><td>35元</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">本表及收費規則均爲原創模擬情境。</p></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>1. Which room satisfies all three requirements of Lin’s group?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Qinghe Room.</p><p><b>B.</b> Yulin Room.</p><p><b>C.</b> Songfeng Room.</p><p><b>D.</b> Zhuying Room.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>2. When should a first-time user arrive for a 10:00 reservation?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> At 09:50 for the equipment orientation.</p><p><b>B.</b> At 10:05 after the clearing period has finished.</p><p><b>C.</b> At 10:00 because instruction is included in the booked time.</p><p><b>D.</b> At 09:00 because every room requires an hour of preparation.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>3. How does the speaker clarify the twenty-yuan charge?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It pays for the session and equipment service rather than a refundable deposit.</p><p><b>B.</b> It applies only when the room’s maximum capacity is exceeded.</p><p><b>C.</b> It buys a permanent copy of the room’s equipment.</p><p><b>D.</b> It is a deposit automatically returned after any session.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>4. What must be allowed for within a sixty-minute reservation?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> An extra ten-minute orientation after the recording ends.</p><p><b>B.</b> A free extension whenever the recording needs more time.</p><p><b>C.</b> A mandatory sixty minutes of uninterrupted recording.</p><p><b>D.</b> Five minutes for clearing up before the reserved period ends.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>5. Why should users verify a personal copy of their files before leaving?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To increase the room’s permitted number of people.</p><p><b>B.</b> To avoid relying on a public computer that will be cleared.</p><p><b>C.</b> To turn a session charge into a refundable deposit.</p><p><b>D.</b> To replace the equipment orientation with self-study.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L2 · Questions 6–10</p><h2>Making a uniform exchange usable</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l2.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l2.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>6. What change is central to this year’s exchange?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Requiring each student to donate before being allowed to try anything.</p><p><b>B.</b> Collecting every donated garment without checking its condition.</p><p><b>C.</b> Focusing on matching usable clothes to actual needs and access.</p><p><b>D.</b> Replacing all size information with a count of stored items.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>7. Why are measurements recorded instead of relying solely on letter-size labels?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Labels may correspond to different cuts in different years.</p><p><b>B.</b> All students have requested the same size.</p><p><b>C.</b> The school wants to stop students from trying clothes on.</p><p><b>D.</b> Measuring a garment automatically repairs any damage.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>8. What concern explains the decision not to ask about household income?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The organizers need to prove every participant has the same income.</p><p><b>B.</b> Only people receiving formal assistance are allowed to collect clothes.</p><p><b>C.</b> Every participant must explain why a new uniform is unaffordable.</p><p><b>D.</b> People should be able to use the exchange without disclosing private circumstances.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>9. What problem does the added evening period address?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A clash between the old collection time and some students’ classes.</p><p><b>B.</b> The inability to measure clothes in daylight.</p><p><b>C.</b> The need to increase the permitted size of donated coats.</p><p><b>D.</b> A requirement that every student attend twice.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>10. What will guide the next request for donations?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The sizes and types still needed by potential recipients.</p><p><b>B.</b> The number of boxes that can be stacked in the storeroom.</p><p><b>C.</b> A rule that each family must donate the same garment.</p><p><b>D.</b> A target to exceed last year’s total regardless of fit.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L3 · Questions 11–15</p><h2>An invitation is not yet a confirmation</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l3.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l3.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>11. What is the initial misunderstanding between the students?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> One assumes the event has already been cancelled.</p><p><b>B.</b> One thinks the teacher has requested a different activity.</p><p><b>C.</b> One believes the teacher has asked to prepare all the seats.</p><p><b>D.</b> One treats a tentative reply as a definite agreement.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>12. Why does the male speaker consider a follow-up question appropriate?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It removes the need to explain the planning situation.</p><p><b>B.</b> It can force the teacher to choose the students’ preferred time.</p><p><b>C.</b> A casual invitation always requires an immediate answer.</p><p><b>D.</b> The organizers have a practical reason to clarify attendance.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>13. What does “留个余地” mean in the suggested message?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Reserve extra floor space inside the activity room.</p><p><b>B.</b> Allow the teacher to say that a decision is not yet possible.</p><p><b>C.</b> Remove all deadlines and stop planning the event.</p><p><b>D.</b> Assume the teacher will attend unless told otherwise.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>14. Which message best follows the speakers’ proposal?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> “Please attend at our chosen time without further discussion.”</p><p><b>B.</b> “We have confirmed your place because you did not refuse.”</p><p><b>C.</b> “Would morning or afternoon work? If you are still unsure, please let us know.”</p><p><b>D.</b> “Your tentative reply means we have removed you permanently.”</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>15. What principle concludes the discussion?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Once a name is written down, later changes should be ignored.</p><p><b>B.</b> Clear arrangements and respect for another person’s choice can support each other.</p><p><b>C.</b> Organizers should interpret every ambiguous message on the sender’s behalf.</p><p><b>D.</b> The most polite response is always the most certain response.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L4 · Questions 16–20</p><h2>Keeping a classroom dataset interpretable</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l4.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l4.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>16. How should a blank entry in the usage-count column be interpreted?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> As proof that the service was used exactly once.</p><p><b>B.</b> As the average number of uses among other respondents.</p><p><b>C.</b> As an unanswered item rather than a recorded zero.</p><p><b>D.</b> As a duplicate that must always be deleted.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>17. Before combining waiting-time files, what must students check?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Whether every blank can be replaced with the largest value.</p><p><b>B.</b> Whether the recorded values use minutes or seconds.</p><p><b>C.</b> Whether the original file can be discarded to save space.</p><p><b>D.</b> Whether all participants have identical identification numbers.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>18. Why can two rows with the same participant number both be valid?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> They may describe visits on different dates.</p><p><b>B.</b> The file requires identical copies of every response.</p><p><b>C.</b> The number alone proves the rows concern different people.</p><p><b>D.</b> The participant must have entered the same date twice.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>19. Why is filtering preferred when comparing first-time visitors?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It permanently removes all missing responses.</p><p><b>B.</b> It makes the source units automatically correct.</p><p><b>C.</b> It preserves other records for later questions.</p><p><b>D.</b> It changes repeat visits into first visits.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>20. What information should accompany the final summary?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A claim that every unanswered item has been inferred correctly.</p><p><b>B.</b> A statement that records with matching IDs were always removed.</p><p><b>C.</b> Only the largest total, with all processing steps hidden.</p><p><b>D.</b> An account of included records and the treatment of nonresponses.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">listening · L5 · Questions 21–25</p><h2>A family learns to keep in touch differently</h2><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l5.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><audio controls preload="none" src="assets/audio/l5.mp3"></audio><div class="cn"></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>21. Which assumption does the speaker reconsider?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Only people living in the same city can stay connected.</p><p><b>B.</b> A fixed call must be cancelled whenever one person is busy.</p><p><b>C.</b> Family members should never use recorded voice messages.</p><p><b>D.</b> Longer simultaneous calls necessarily indicate a closer relationship.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>22. What does the speaker learn about delayed replies?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> They always mean the sender’s message has been rejected.</p><p><b>B.</b> They require every family message to become urgent.</p><p><b>C.</b> They may reflect practical circumstances rather than a lack of concern.</p><p><b>D.</b> They prove that recorded messages cannot be understood.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>23. Which arrangement helps the grandmother participate?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Replacing her account with someone else’s summary.</p><p><b>B.</b> Leaving room for a slower speaker during the shared call.</p><p><b>C.</b> Giving the fastest speaker every available minute.</p><p><b>D.</b> Requiring all stories to be sent in writing only.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>24. In this account, what does “惦记” most nearly express?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Keeping someone in mind with care and attention.</p><p><b>B.</b> Demanding an immediate response to every message.</p><p><b>C.</b> Counting how many minutes another person stays online.</p><p><b>D.</b> Checking whether everyone has the same work schedule.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>25. What is the presentation’s concluding recommendation?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Avoid setting any response deadline even for time-sensitive matters.</p><p><b>B.</b> Adopt the same one-hour rule for every household.</p><p><b>C.</b> Replace discussion with a ranking of message totals.</p><p><b>D.</b> Discuss communication methods that let family members feel heard.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R1 · Questions 26–30</p><h2>Before the ending is discussed</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 邮件一｜读书会协调员致主持人许南
许南你好！周五晚的线上短篇读书会原定四十五分钟，每个人先谈结局，再选最喜欢的人物。新加入的几位成员只读到一半，担心提前知道情节就失去阅读的乐趣。请你在周四前提出调整方案，既保持讨论深度，也让未读完的人能参加。总时长仍为四十五分钟。</p><p><b>p2</b> 邮件二｜许南回复
老师您好！我建议前二十五分钟只讨论前半段，每个人选一处描写提出问题，不要求先读完全篇。后面二十分钟再进入自愿参加的结局讨论；没读完的成员可以先离开，或者在不涉及结局的留言区谈感受。这样不必因为进度不同就取消参加。</p><p><b>p3</b> 开场时，我会明确说明两个阶段，并在引导页上标出哪些链接涉及“剧透”。留言区也分开，避免结局内容混进去。我可以制作这张引导页并提前核对链接。还有一件事想确认：大家手里的版本页码是否一致？如果不同，我们用段落首句定位，而不要求每个人报同一页数。讨论需要共同的位置，但不一定需要完全相同的阅读速度。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 郵件一｜讀書會協調員致主持人許南
許南你好！週五晚的線上短篇讀書會原定四十五分鐘，每個人先談結局，再選最喜歡的人物。新加入的幾位成員只讀到一半，擔心提前知道情節就失去閱讀的樂趣。請你在週四前提出調整方案，既保持討論深度，也讓未讀完的人能參加。總時長仍爲四十五分鐘。</p><p><b>p2</b> 郵件二｜許南迴復
老師您好！我建議前二十五分鐘只討論前半段，每個人選一處描寫提出問題，不要求先讀完全篇。後面二十分鐘再進入自願參加的結局討論；沒讀完的成員可以先離開，或者在不涉及結局的留言區談感受。這樣不必因爲進度不同就取消參加。</p><p><b>p3</b> 開場時，我會明確說明兩個階段，並在引導頁上標出哪些鏈接涉及“劇透”。留言區也分開，避免結局內容混進去。我可以製作這張引導頁並提前核對鏈接。還有一件事想確認：大家手裏的版本頁碼是否一致？如果不同，我們用段落首句定位，而不要求每個人報同一頁數。討論需要共同的位置，但不一定需要完全相同的閱讀速度。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>26. Why is a revised format requested for the book club?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To remove all discussion of characters from future meetings.</p><p><b>B.</b> To include readers at different stages without prematurely revealing the ending.</p><p><b>C.</b> To require unfinished readers to stop reading until the event ends.</p><p><b>D.</b> To extend the meeting beyond its original scheduled duration.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>27. What is proposed for the first twenty-five minutes?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Require everyone to explain the final scene in turn.</p><p><b>B.</b> Discuss selected descriptions from the first half of the story.</p><p><b>C.</b> Ask only members who have completed the story to speak.</p><p><b>D.</b> Use the whole period to vote on whether the book should be finished.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>28. What does “剧透” refer to on the proposed guide page?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A technical failure that prevents an online discussion from opening.</p><p><b>B.</b> A request to replace written comments with spoken answers.</p><p><b>C.</b> Information that reveals story developments before a reader reaches them.</p><p><b>D.</b> An alternative edition with a different page size.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>29. Which responsibility does Xu Nan offer to take on?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Prepare a guide page and verify the relevant links in advance.</p><p><b>B.</b> Choose one official interpretation that every reader must accept.</p><p><b>C.</b> Rewrite the ending so all editions use the same page numbers.</p><p><b>D.</b> Cancel the later discussion whenever one member leaves early.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>30. Why does Xu Nan ask whether everyone’s edition has matching page numbers?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To choose a reliable way for participants to locate the same passage.</p><p><b>B.</b> To exclude editions printed in a different typeface.</p><p><b>C.</b> To ensure the later segment lasts longer than the first one.</p><p><b>D.</b> To judge which reader has bought the most expensive version.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R2 · Questions 31–35</p><h2>A shorter label is not the whole answer</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 校园科学展的旧版说明牌文字很多，参观者常在中途离开。展览小组制作了新版：增加步骤图，把长段落改成短标题和分段说明。试用后，有人说：“既然看完的人多了，以后所有说明只要越短越好。”小组没有马上接受这个结论。</p><p><b>p2</b> 他们在两个不同开放日各邀请一百位参观者填写简短问卷，询问是否读到说明末尾，并记录至少提过一个问题的人数。表中“完成阅读”来自参观者自报，不是理解能力测验。同一人可能既报告读完，也提出问题，不能把两列人数直接相加当作不同人的总数。</p><p><b>p3</b> 新版的两项人数都上升了。记录提问内容的同学发现，有些新问题是“我能不能换一个材料再试”，不只是“这句话什么意思”。因此，提问增加未必说明介绍更难懂，也可能说明有人开始想继续探索。不过，新版同时改变了长度、结构和图片，不能单独认定只是字数减少造成了变化。</p><p><b>p4</b> 下一轮，小组打算保留关键步骤和必要解释，再测试不同排版，而不是不断删字。报告也会说明两个开放日的人并非同一批，不能据此保证每位参观者都更理解。清楚的说明应让人知道怎样继续，而不只是更快走到最后。</p></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>科学展览的两版说明牌记录</caption><thead><tr><th>版本</th><th>受访人数</th><th>自报完成阅读</th><th>至少提问一次</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>旧版</td><td>100人</td><td>60人</td><td>20人</td></tr><tr><td>新版</td><td>100人</td><td>82人</td><td>35人</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">原创模拟数据；完成阅读为自报，两个开放日的受访者不同。</p><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 校園科學展的舊版說明牌文字很多，參觀者常在中途離開。展覽小組製作了新版：增加步驟圖，把長段落改成短標題和分段說明。試用後，有人說：“既然看完的人多了，以後所有說明只要越短越好。”小組沒有馬上接受這個結論。</p><p><b>p2</b> 他們在兩個不同開放日各邀請一百位參觀者填寫簡短問卷，詢問是否讀到說明末尾，並記錄至少提過一個問題的人數。表中“完成閱讀”來自參觀者自報，不是理解能力測驗。同一人可能既報告讀完，也提出問題，不能把兩列人數直接相加當作不同人的總數。</p><p><b>p3</b> 新版的兩項人數都上升了。記錄提問內容的同學發現，有些新問題是“我能不能換一個材料再試”，不只是“這句話什麼意思”。因此，提問增加未必說明介紹更難懂，也可能說明有人開始想繼續探索。不過，新版同時改變了長度、結構和圖片，不能單獨認定只是字數減少造成了變化。</p><p><b>p4</b> 下一輪，小組打算保留關鍵步驟和必要解釋，再測試不同排版，而不是不斷刪字。報告也會說明兩個開放日的人並非同一批，不能據此保證每位參觀者都更理解。清楚的說明應讓人知道怎樣繼續，而不只是更快走到最後。</p></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>科學展覽的兩版說明牌記錄</caption><thead><tr><th>版本</th><th>受訪人數</th><th>自報完成閱讀</th><th>至少提問一次</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>舊版</td><td>100人</td><td>60人</td><td>20人</td></tr><tr><td>新版</td><td>100人</td><td>82人</td><td>35人</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="muted">原創模擬數據；完成閱讀爲自報，兩個開放日的受訪者不同。</p></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>31. What limits the interpretation of the “completed reading” figures?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> They refer to the number of words printed on each label.</p><p><b>B.</b> They count only the people who asked exactly one question.</p><p><b>C.</b> They prove that every visitor understood the experiment equally well.</p><p><b>D.</b> They report what visitors said, rather than directly measuring comprehension.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>32. What change is shown for visitors who asked at least one question?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The figure rose from twenty to thirty-five.</p><p><b>B.</b> The figure remained the same across both versions.</p><p><b>C.</b> The figure fell from thirty-five to twenty.</p><p><b>D.</b> Every visitor in both groups asked a question.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>33. Why should the two new-version columns not be added to count distinct visitors?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Every question automatically cancels a completed-reading response.</p><p><b>B.</b> The table contains no record of how many people were surveyed.</p><p><b>C.</b> The same person may appear in both categories.</p><p><b>D.</b> The two columns describe different schools in different cities.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>34. What does the example about trying a different material suggest?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The number of questions alone proves the experiment is unsafe.</p><p><b>B.</b> Some questions may show a wish to explore beyond the initial explanation.</p><p><b>C.</b> All questions arise because the instructions are unreadable.</p><p><b>D.</b> The new labels eliminated any need for further investigation.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>35. What prevents the group from attributing the results solely to fewer words?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The group never recorded either reading completion or questions.</p><p><b>B.</b> The survey excluded all visitors who read the new labels.</p><p><b>C.</b> The new version used exactly the old wording and layout.</p><p><b>D.</b> Length, structure and imagery changed together.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R3 · Questions 36–40</p><h2>Whose recipe counts as local?</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 社区文化节筹备组想做一块“本地味道”展板，请三位居民介绍家里常做的拌面。第一位喜欢加酸，第二位把蔬菜切得很细，第三位搬来不久，保留了原来家乡的一种配料。整理资料的学生发现，三个人都把自己的做法叫作“家常”，却没有一份完全相同的食谱。</p><p><b>p2</b> 起初，有人建议投票选出最正宗的一份，其他做法就不写了。居民杨阿姨问：“我们要介绍大家怎样生活，还是要评出谁做得对？”她说，自己减去一种配料，是因为家人的口味变了，并不表示忘记了以前怎样做。另一位居民也提醒，某一家人的习惯不能自动代表整个地方。</p><p><b>p3</b> 学生于是改变整理方式：每份做法都写明提供者、他在什么情况下做，以及本人怎样解释这个选择。他们把“居民说这是母亲教的”与“这就是全城最古老的做法”分开，后一句如果没有其他资料，就不放进说明。记录个人记忆有价值，但不能让记忆承担它没有证明的结论。</p><p><b>p4</b> 最终展板保留了三种做法，并邀请观众补充自己家的变化。筹备组没有说所有变化都一样，也没有把不同直接排成高低。所谓本地生活，不只是找到一份永远不变的标准答案，还可以看见住在这里的人怎样延续、调整和交流日常经验。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 社區文化節籌備組想做一塊“本地味道”展板，請三位居民介紹家裏常做的拌麪。第一位喜歡加酸，第二位把蔬菜切得很細，第三位搬來不久，保留了原來家鄉的一種配料。整理資料的學生髮現，三個人都把自己的做法叫作“家常”，卻沒有一份完全相同的食譜。</p><p><b>p2</b> 起初，有人建議投票選出最正宗的一份，其他做法就不寫了。居民楊阿姨問：“我們要介紹大家怎樣生活，還是要評出誰做得對？”她說，自己減去一種配料，是因爲家人的口味變了，並不表示忘記了以前怎樣做。另一位居民也提醒，某一家人的習慣不能自動代表整個地方。</p><p><b>p3</b> 學生於是改變整理方式：每份做法都寫明提供者、他在什麼情況下做，以及本人怎樣解釋這個選擇。他們把“居民說這是母親教的”與“這就是全城最古老的做法”分開，後一句如果沒有其他資料，就不放進說明。記錄個人記憶有價值，但不能讓記憶承擔它沒有證明的結論。</p><p><b>p4</b> 最終展板保留了三種做法，並邀請觀衆補充自己家的變化。籌備組沒有說所有變化都一樣，也沒有把不同直接排成高低。所謂本地生活，不只是找到一份永遠不變的標準答案，還可以看見住在這裏的人怎樣延續、調整和交流日常經驗。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>36. Which view of local food does the article develop?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Everyday food traditions can include variation and change among residents.</p><p><b>B.</b> A recipe has cultural value only when no ingredient has ever changed.</p><p><b>C.</b> Residents who have moved recently cannot contribute to community life.</p><p><b>D.</b> One household’s recipe should permanently replace all other versions.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>37. Why does the revised display identify who supplied each recipe?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To guarantee that every contributor has lived there for the same number of years.</p><p><b>B.</b> To let viewers rank families solely by the number of ingredients.</p><p><b>C.</b> To connect an account to a particular person and context rather than imply universal agreement.</p><p><b>D.</b> To avoid explaining the circumstances in which the food is prepared.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>38. What concern does Yang raise about choosing the “最正宗” version by vote?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Voting would make the exhibition too long to print.</p><p><b>B.</b> The vote could turn an account of varied lives into a judgment of who is right.</p><p><b>C.</b> The residents have all forgotten how their families used to cook.</p><p><b>D.</b> A majority vote would automatically establish the oldest recipe in the city.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>39. Which statement best respects the evidence rule adopted by the students?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> “All families must have changed the recipe for exactly the same reason.”</p><p><b>B.</b> “This resident says she learned this version from her mother.”</p><p><b>C.</b> “One contributor’s memory establishes the age of every local recipe.”</p><p><b>D.</b> “This account proves that no other version existed anywhere in the city.”</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>40. How would the organizers most likely respond to a fourth resident’s different recipe?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Invite an explanation of that resident’s practice and include its context.</p><p><b>B.</b> Reject it solely because it differs from the most common version.</p><p><b>C.</b> Remove all previous accounts immediately.</p><p><b>D.</b> Describe it as the city’s original recipe without checking sources.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R4 · Questions 41–45</p><h2>Learning to catch a missed line</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 第一次排练，我把剧本背得很熟。对手演员每说一句，我就在心里数着还差几个字轮到自己。老师让我们停下来，说我虽然没念错，却像在等闹钟响。我不服气：台词都对了，还能有什么问题？</p><p><b>p2</b> 下一次，我突然忘了一个词，脑子里只剩下剧本上空白的一块。小周没有停下来提醒“你忘词了”，而是沿着角色的口气问：“你刚才不是还想把这封信交给她吗？”我看见他手里的信，想起人物此时要做的事，顺着情节说出了下一句。</p><p><b>p3</b> 老师说，接住同伴，不是随便加话，更不是替对方把整场戏演完。我们重新核对关键情节，约定可能用到的提示，也认真练习原来的台词。我这才明白，准备并没有变得不重要，只是不能把准备理解为关上耳朵、只等自己的那一段。</p><p><b>p4</b> 正式演出时，小周比平时多停了一会儿。我没有急着用自己的句子填满安静，而是看着他，等他把信慢慢放到桌上，才接着说。那一刻，我听到的不只是下一句台词的开头，也听到了一个人正在作出的选择。谢幕以后，我仍把剧本收得整整齐齐，只是不再认为，背得最像录音的人就一定演得最好。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 第一次排練，我把劇本背得很熟。對手演員每說一句，我就在心裏數着還差幾個字輪到自己。老師讓我們停下來，說我雖然沒念錯，卻像在等鬧鐘響。我不服氣：臺詞都對了，還能有什麼問題？</p><p><b>p2</b> 下一次，我突然忘了一個詞，腦子裏只剩下劇本上空白的一塊。小周沒有停下來提醒“你忘詞了”，而是沿着角色的口氣問：“你剛纔不是還想把這封信交給她嗎？”我看見他手裏的信，想起人物此時要做的事，順着情節說出了下一句。</p><p><b>p3</b> 老師說，接住同伴，不是隨便加話，更不是替對方把整場戲演完。我們重新覈對關鍵情節，約定可能用到的提示，也認真練習原來的臺詞。我這才明白，準備並沒有變得不重要，只是不能把準備理解爲關上耳朵、只等自己的那一段。</p><p><b>p4</b> 正式演出時，小周比平時多停了一會兒。我沒有急着用自己的句子填滿安靜，而是看着他，等他把信慢慢放到桌上，才接着說。那一刻，我聽到的不只是下一句臺詞的開頭，也聽到了一個人正在作出的選擇。謝幕以後，我仍把劇本收得整整齊齊，只是不再認爲，背得最像錄音的人就一定演得最好。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>41. During the first rehearsal, what occupies the narrator’s attention?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> How the audience will interpret the final bow.</p><p><b>B.</b> The moment when it will be time to recite the next memorized line.</p><p><b>C.</b> Ways to remove all pauses from the other actor’s speech.</p><p><b>D.</b> Whether the letter prop has been placed on the table.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>42. How does Xiao Zhou help when the narrator forgets a word?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> He stops the performance to announce the narrator’s mistake to everyone.</p><p><b>B.</b> He offers a question within the scene that reconnects the narrator with the character’s purpose.</p><p><b>C.</b> He discards the letter so that the original plot cannot continue.</p><p><b>D.</b> He reads all of the narrator’s remaining lines without allowing a response.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>43. What does “接住同伴” mean in the teacher’s explanation?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Physically carry an actor across the stage.</p><p><b>B.</b> Repeat every word regardless of what the partner does.</p><p><b>C.</b> Respond supportively while maintaining the shared scene and its purpose.</p><p><b>D.</b> Take over the entire role so the partner never has to speak.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>44. How does the narrator’s understanding of preparation change?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> All rehearsals are cancelled because one successful prompt is enough.</p><p><b>B.</b> Memorizing any dialogue is now considered harmful.</p><p><b>C.</b> Actors are encouraged to replace the agreed plot with unrelated improvisation.</p><p><b>D.</b> Preparation comes to include listening and coordinating as well as knowing the script.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>45. What does the narrator’s response to the pause during the performance reveal?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> A new willingness to attend to the partner’s meaningful action.</p><p><b>B.</b> A wish to make the partner seem less prepared.</p><p><b>C.</b> An inability to remember where the letter belongs.</p><p><b>D.</b> A decision to abandon the script permanently.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R5 · Questions 46–50</p><h2>An invitation to build a sound portrait</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 校园广播站征集｜我身边的声音
一把钥匙开门的声音、一段练习中的乐句，或每天熟悉的另一种声音，都可能带出一个故事。请提交一段六十至九十秒的原创录音，并配一百至一百五十字中文说明，介绍声音来自哪里、为什么值得听。普通手机就能参与，不要求专业器材。</p><p><b>p2</b> 本次征集的规则是：录到可辨认的他人谈话前，请先获得对方同意；无法确认时，换一个不含可辨认谈话的素材。不要把网上下载的声音当成自己的作品。提交文件可用 MP3 或 WAV，并附上作者希望使用的署名。</p><p><b>p3</b> 截止时间为五月五日十八点。系统显示“已收到”只表示文件提交成功，不代表已被选播。若文件不能打开，工作人员会通知作者，作者仍须在截止前补交可播放版本。我们将于五月十二日发送反馈；入选片段会在作者确认署名后用于本次校园广播专题。</p><p><b>p4</b> 评选关注声音与说明怎样共同表现一个具体经验，而不是谁录得最响，或设备最贵。请保留原始文件，并在说明最后留下可联系的校内邮箱。以上时间与规则仅用于这项原创模拟征集。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 校園廣播站徵集｜我身邊的聲音
一把鑰匙開門的聲音、一段練習中的樂句，或每天熟悉的另一種聲音，都可能帶出一個故事。請提交一段六十至九十秒的原創錄音，並配一百至一百五十字中文說明，介紹聲音來自哪裏、爲什麼值得聽。普通手機就能參與，不要求專業器材。</p><p><b>p2</b> 本次徵集的規則是：錄到可辨認的他人談話前，請先獲得對方同意；無法確認時，換一個不含可辨認談話的素材。不要把網上下載的聲音當成自己的作品。提交文件可用 MP3 或 WAV，並附上作者希望使用的署名。</p><p><b>p3</b> 截止時間爲五月五日十八點。系統顯示“已收到”只表示文件提交成功，不代表已被選播。若文件不能打開，工作人員會通知作者，作者仍須在截止前補交可播放版本。我們將於五月十二日發送反饋；入選片段會在作者確認署名後用於本次校園廣播專題。</p><p><b>p4</b> 評選關注聲音與說明怎樣共同表現一個具體經驗，而不是誰錄得最響，或設備最貴。請保留原始文件，並在說明最後留下可聯繫的校內郵箱。以上時間與規則僅用於這項原創模擬徵集。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>46. What is the central aim of the sound submission?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To measure which student can produce the loudest noise.</p><p><b>B.</b> To compare the prices of professional recording devices.</p><p><b>C.</b> To use an original sound and contextual writing to convey a particular experience.</p><p><b>D.</b> To reproduce an online recording with a different filename.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>47. What should a student do if permission for identifiable conversation cannot be confirmed?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Assume a successful upload supplies the missing permission.</p><p><b>B.</b> Replace the contributor’s name with the name of the broadcaster.</p><p><b>C.</b> Submit it anonymously and omit the contextual explanation.</p><p><b>D.</b> Choose material without identifiable conversation instead.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>48. How are May 5 and May 12 related in the notice?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The first is a rehearsal date; the second is the deadline for initial entries.</p><p><b>B.</b> Both are interchangeable dates for guaranteed broadcasting.</p><p><b>C.</b> May 12 automatically extends every unreadable file’s deadline.</p><p><b>D.</b> The first is the submission deadline; the second is the planned feedback date.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>49. If a submitted audio file cannot be opened, what does the notice require?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Wait until after feedback and assume the original entry has been selected.</p><p><b>B.</b> Buy professional equipment and abandon the original recording.</p><p><b>C.</b> Provide a playable replacement before the submission deadline.</p><p><b>D.</b> Send only the written explanation after the deadline.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>50. Which conclusion is supported by an “已收到” message?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The author no longer needs to confirm the intended credit.</p><p><b>B.</b> The file was submitted, while selection is still undecided.</p><p><b>C.</b> The entry will be broadcast regardless of its content.</p><p><b>D.</b> The piece has won because its recording is louder than others.</p></div></article></section><section class="box"><p class="eyebrow">reading · R6 · Questions 51–55</p><h2>Making a changed meeting point clear</h2><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 消息一｜活动协调员请学生检查通知
小禾，集合点临时换了，我准备在群里写：“明天照常，换到新地点，晚到自己找队伍。”老成员应该知道新地点在哪里。请你看看，新加入的成员能不能据此安排行程。报名和分工都不变，只改集合位置。</p><p><b>p2</b> 消息二｜小禾回复
老师，我建议直接写：“六月十三日十四点在体育馆东门集合，十四点十分出发。原主楼北门因施工停用。本次报名和分工不变。迟到者请先联系协调员确认队伍位置，不要独自追赶。”这样第一次参加的人也能找到入口，而不必猜大家说的新地点是哪儿。</p><p><b>p3</b> 我把“明天”换成具体日期，是因为有人可能隔天才看到转发。请您确认这份版本后，我们可以把它置顶，并在旧通知下标明“集合点已更新，请看置顶”。只转发一张没有日期的旧截图，容易让不同成员拿着不同信息出门。我愿意核对群里的最终版本，但地点是否正式可用，还需要您向场地负责人确认。</p></div><details><summary>Traditional Chinese source / 繁體材料</summary><div class="cn"><p><b>p1</b> 消息一｜活動協調員請學生檢查通知
小禾，集合點臨時換了，我準備在羣裏寫：“明天照常，換到新地點，晚到自己找隊伍。”老成員應該知道新地點在哪裏。請你看看，新加入的成員能不能據此安排行程。報名和分工都不變，只改集合位置。</p><p><b>p2</b> 消息二｜小禾回覆
老師，我建議直接寫：“六月十三日十四點在體育館東門集合，十四點十分出發。原主樓北門因施工停用。本次報名和分工不變。遲到者請先聯繫協調員確認隊伍位置，不要獨自追趕。”這樣第一次參加的人也能找到入口，而不必猜大家說的新地點是哪兒。</p><p><b>p3</b> 我把“明天”換成具體日期，是因爲有人可能隔天纔看到轉發。請您確認這份版本後，我們可以把它置頂，並在舊通知下標明“集合點已更新，請看置頂”。只轉發一張沒有日期的舊截圖，容易讓不同成員拿着不同信息出門。我願意覈對羣裏的最終版本，但地點是否正式可用，還需要您向場地負責人確認。</p></div></details><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>51. Which arrangements are explicitly unchanged?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> The use of the main building’s north entrance.</p><p><b>B.</b> The location named in the earlier notice.</p><p><b>C.</b> Registration and assigned responsibilities.</p><p><b>D.</b> Every participant’s personal travel route.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>52. Why does Xiao He replace “tomorrow” with a calendar date?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> To avoid stating any precise departure time.</p><p><b>B.</b> To make the old entrance usable again.</p><p><b>C.</b> To indicate that the event will happen every day.</p><p><b>D.</b> To keep the message clear when it is read or forwarded later.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>53. What should a late participant do under the revised instructions?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Head alone toward where the group might have gone.</p><p><b>B.</b> Assume registration and responsibilities have been cancelled.</p><p><b>C.</b> Contact the coordinator first to establish the group’s location.</p><p><b>D.</b> Return to the closed north entrance and wait indefinitely.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>54. How does Xiao He suggest handling outdated notices?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> Mark the update on the old notice and direct readers to the pinned version.</p><p><b>B.</b> Let each member choose whichever meeting point seems familiar.</p><p><b>C.</b> Keep forwarding undated screenshots as the primary instruction.</p><p><b>D.</b> Erase every discussion of the change without posting a final version.</p></div></article><article class="item" style="border-top:1px solid #dbe3ed;padding-top:22px;margin-top:22px"><h3>55. What makes the revised announcement more useful to a first-time participant?</h3><div class="options"><p><b>A.</b> It replaces confirmed venue information with a familiar old screenshot.</p><p><b>B.</b> It gives a specific place, date, timing, and a procedure for an exception.</p><p><b>C.</b> It assumes every reader already knows what “the new place” means.</p><p><b>D.</b> It removes the distinction between arrival and departure.</p></div></article></section></main></body></html>